    "question": {
      ":question/id": "3cc4e81f-06d7-5307-bc26-07428c65f61d",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are seventh-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${x}^{9}{y}^{\u2032}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}={y}^{\u2033}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}})^{2}$\n- **II.** ${y}^{\u2032}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}})^{4}={x}^{8}{y}^{\u2032}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$\n- **III.** ${x}^{10}{y}^{\u2032}+y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}={y}^{\u2033}(\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}})^{4}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A differential equation is of seventh-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a seventh-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}},$ so this is a eighth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a seventh-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{5}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{5}},$ so this is a fifth-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is not a seventh-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}},$ so this is a sixth-order differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"none.\"",
